<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Auch</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Elimberris, Augusta Auscorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Auch is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gers">Gers</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. Called Elimberris by the Aquitanian Ausci and Augusta Auscorum by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, it became the seat of the archbishops of Gascony in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Its Gothic cathedral of Sainte-Marie is known for its carved choir stalls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> stained glass.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/auch.jpg" alt="Auch" width="320" height="286" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: en:User:Hardouin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Auch1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
